4 #edb/ref 1061 30972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162904 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2a1d5d86-333c-5361-87ec-fd7766797988" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162904 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162905 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162905 #edb/ref 1061 30973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162905 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "86248bdd-6a75-54cd-8196-8b333a64cac5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162905 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163224 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $\\mathbb{R}.$ The **spectral theorem** states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- The eigenvectors corresponding to *different* eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163224 #edb/ref 1065 4882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163224 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5ecbbdd0-25a0-52ee-8f0c-987dcd8ba584" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163224 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163243 #edb/ref 1064 "We can write an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix $A$ in terms of a sum of products of its eigenvalues and eigenvectors.\n\nSpecifically, if $\\mathbf{u}_i$ denotes an orthonormal eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_i$ for $i=1,\\ldots, n$, then\n\n$$\nA=\\sum_{i=1}^n \\lambda_i \\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T.\n$$\n\nExpressing a symmetric matrix $A$ in this way is called the **spectral decomposition** of $A.$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the spectral decomposition of the $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/40/408e7471e4ef788758d423af97f5683186f79192fd8933c350d45bb87f089875.svg)\n\nThe eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1 = 6$ and $\\lambda_2 = -4,$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are\n\n![](images/0c/0c22f7dd97e83c358cb3cef1fcc2db902d908140109334a8cf1c22b35b29c6d2.svg)\n\nThe spectral decomposition of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA =\\sum_{i=1}^2 \\lambda_i \\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T = \\lambda_1 \\mathbf u_1 \\mathbf u_1^T + \\lambda_2 \\mathbf u_2 \\mathbf u_2^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_1$ and $\\mathbf u_2$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spectral decomposition of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{6}_{⏟}}_{{\\lambda }_{1}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{10} & \\frac{3}{10} \\\\ \\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{9}{10}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}}+{{(-4)}_{⏟}}_{{\\lambda }_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9}{10} & -\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this decomposition, the matrix $\\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T$ is the orthogonal projection matrix that maps any $\\mathbf x \\in \\mathbb R^2$ onto the subspace spanned by $\\mathbf u_i.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186163243 #edb/ref 1065 4973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163243 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a90957e3-e4b9-548d-930b-e060c6b63859" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163243 #edb/ref 1067 "The Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366581 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366581 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366581 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366581 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366581 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366581 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366581 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366581 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186366586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366581 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f4531768-c85a-5927-b9ea-dd6e8018f412" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366582 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186366582 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366582 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f9efaaf0-779f-5a72-aa61-4761f6295dad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366583 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186366583 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366583 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5d20e65d-8c96-5a7e-8fea-9024d97dc82f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366584 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186366584 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366584 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d2283d99-cd32-55e5-9365-da3163600171" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366585 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -8\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186366585 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366585 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1172809a-fec9-5ba9-a260-4a0f08543d35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366586 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186366586 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366586 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d89d3c9b-2928-5750-a596-9fb110e7f349" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366636 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452946 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366636 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452947 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366636 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452948 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366636 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95405" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366636 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366636 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $A$ is an $8\\times 8$ real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable\n- **II.** $A$ must have $8$ real and distinct eigenvalues\n- **III.** If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u⊥v$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366636 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "66a9cbb1-7460-5af7-96d5-cd936a2ef154" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366636 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the spectral theorem, the eigenvalues are real but not necessarily distinct. This occurs when the characteristic polynomial has roots whose (algebraic) multiplicity is greater than $1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366650 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452952 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366650 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452953 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366650 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452954 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366650 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95700" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366650 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366650 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable\n- **II.** $A$ must have $36$ real eigenvalues\n- **III.** If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors, then $u$ is parallel to $v$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366650 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0baadb2d-d441-5a17-add5-c8350f576311" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366650 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the spectral theorem, the eigenvalues are real but there are not $36$ of them. An $n\\times n$ matrix has at most $n$ distinct eigenvalues.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal